From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 18:59:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: input comes from logind, not from the input group

On a tty the desktop came up deaf. `InputDeviceOpenerEvdev::OpenInputDevice`
opens `/dev/input/event*` with a bare `open(O_RDWR | O_NONBLOCK)`, and on an
ordinary Linux desktop that is `Permission denied`: logind's
`70-uaccess.rules` ACLs `drm card*` and `renderD*` and, among input devices,
tags only `ID_INPUT_JOYSTICK`. A keyboard keeps its group-owned mode and the
session user gets no ACL entry on it -- which is exactly the shape of the
first run on real hardware, one `Modeset succeeded` and fifteen `Cannot open
/dev/input/eventN: Permission denied (13)`.

PUTTING THE USER IN THE `input` GROUP IS THE WRONG FIX AND NO OTHER
COMPOSITOR ASKS FOR IT. It is a standing keyboard grant to every process that
user runs, and it is revoked on nothing -- an fd opened while the session was
active goes on delivering after a switch away, which is a keylogger rather
than a papercut. What every Wayland compositor does instead is
`org.freedesktop.login1.Session.TakeControl` and then `TakeDevice(major,
minor)` per device, and the revoke comes free: logind `EVIOCREVOKE`s the fd it
passed when it pauses the device.

THE SEAM ALREADY EXISTED. `InputDeviceOpener` is a one-method pure virtual
that `InputDeviceFactoryEvdev` takes as a constructor argument, so the edits
to files Chromium owns are the choice reaching the evdev thread
(`EventFactoryEvdev` to `EventThreadEvdev` to `EvdevThread`), one protected
`OpenDeviceFd` hook on `InputDeviceOpenerEvdev` -- the descriptor is the one
thing that differs, so the monotonic clock, `EventDeviceInfo` and the choice
of converter stay upstream's -- and the reopen below. Every other platform
keeps the `open`, through a default argument that names it.

CHROMIUM'S OWN `dbus::Bus`, NOT libseat. D-Bus is already compiled into this
engine and already in use by the browser process -- the engine CI log carries
`ERROR:dbus/object_proxy.cc` and `ERROR:dbus/bus.cc` lines from UPower and
NetworkManager calls at runtime -- so this adds no system or third-party
dependency. libseat would add one for no gain. `//ui/ozone/platform/wayland`
already depends on `//dbus` for its idle monitor.

THE THREAD BRIDGE, WHICH IS ONE OF THE TWO HARD PARTS. `OpenInputDevice` is
synchronous and runs on the evdev thread; `CallMethodAndBlock` is only legal
on the thread that owns the bus. The bus is therefore created ON the evdev
thread with a thread-pool single-thread runner of its own, which makes the
evdev thread the bus's ORIGIN thread -- so `ConnectToSignal` and every
`PauseDevice` / `ResumeDevice` callback land there with no hop and no lock,
and the state machine is single-threaded. That leaves exactly one crossing,
`CallMethodAndBlock` posted to the D-Bus thread with this one waiting on a
`base::WaitableEvent`.

Running the bus on the evdev thread with no D-Bus thread at all was the
alternative and it cannot work: `base::Thread` installs a
`FileDescriptorWatcher` only when `CurrentIOThread::IsSet()`
(`base/threading/thread.cc`), the evdev thread is a `MessagePumpType::UI`
pump, and `dbus::Bus` watches its socket through exactly that API. Taking the
devices on the UI thread and handing descriptors across was the other, and it
costs re-plumbing `OpenInputDeviceParams`, `EventFactoryEvdev` and the factory
proxy to carry a descriptor the seam is designed to produce. Blocking is also
what this call already did: the `open` it replaces and the `EVIOCG*` ioctls
behind `EventDeviceInfo::Initialize` are synchronous on this same thread, and
nothing disallows sync primitives on a plain `base::Thread`.

A RESUME IS A REOPEN, WHICH IS THE OTHER HARD PART AND THE ONE THAT WOULD
HAVE SHIPPED A REGRESSION. Swapping the descriptor under the converter looks
like enough and is not. logind `EVIOCREVOKE`s what it paused, the converter's
next read is `ENODEV`, and `EventConverterEvdevImpl` answers that with
`Stop()`; `dup2` cannot re-arm that watch either, because the kernel drops an
epoll registration when the description behind a number is closed, which is
what `dup2` does to the revoked one. `AttachInputDevice` is the ONLY caller of
`EventConverterEvdev::Start()`. So a `ResumeDevice` parks its descriptor
against the device's path and asks the factory for `RemoveInputDevice` then
`AddInputDevice`, and the `OpenInputDevice` that follows consumes the parked
descriptor rather than calling `TakeDevice` again -- logind refuses a second
`TakeDevice` for a device the session already holds, so that part is not
optional. The explicit detach ahead of the open matters too:
`AttachInputDevice` would detach, but a task later, leaving the dead converter
watching in between.

Without it, `0019`'s working `Ctrl+Alt+F<n>` and this would ship together as
"switch away, switch back, keyboard is dead" -- worse than the group
membership they replace, whose descriptors are never revoked at all.

THE FACTORY HANDS THE OPENER ITS WAY BACK IN, from the factory's own
constructor, because an opener is built in order to be given to the factory
and so cannot be given the factory first. `SetDeviceReopener` is not virtual
and the callback lives on `InputDeviceOpener`: an opener that `open`s its own
descriptors never runs it, since nothing takes those away.

THE ID IS REUSED, NOT MINTED. `NextDeviceId()` is a counter on
`EventFactoryEvdev` on the UI thread and is not reachable from here, and
nothing keys on an id being fresh -- `converters_` is keyed by path, and
`OnInputDeviceRemoved(id)` only drops per-device settings for it, which
`AttachInputDevice` re-applies. Reuse is also what happened: it is the same
device coming back. What the device list sees is one removal and one addition
per device per console switch round trip, which is honest about a descriptor
that really was revoked.

TESTED WHERE THE LOGIC IS. `DrmInputDevicesTest`, thirteen cases: the path to
`(major, minor)` through an injected `stat`, including a directory, a block
device carrying the same two numbers in a different space, and a node that
went away between udev's announcement and the lookup; the pause half, where
only type "pause" is answered with `PauseDeviceComplete` -- logind holds the
console switch open until it is, so silence costs its whole timeout -- where a
pause for a device this session never took is answered anyway rather than
wedging the console, and where "gone" forgets the device instead of releasing
it later; the resume half, where the reopen names the same id and path, gets
the descriptor logind sent and gets it exactly once, and where a resume for a
device never taken reopens nothing; and the release, which asks every device
even after one refuses, for the reason `DrmMaster` does -- logind hands a
device to the next session only once every holder has let go.

The reopen double in that suite calls back for the parked descriptor while
`Resume` is still on the stack, which is what the real factory does, so the
re-entrancy is exercised and not merely reasoned about.

`scripts/test-input-comes-from-logind.sh` holds the parts a unit test cannot
reach: that the seam is plugged from `ozone_platform_drm.cc`, that all four
calls and both signals are there, that a resume goes back through the factory
and consumes the parked descriptor BEFORE the call that would take the device
again, and that no `open` came back. It reads the series rather than a
Chromium tree, so it runs in the shell group on every push.

`DrmInputDevicesTest:13` is a floor in both workflows and the filtered run
names the suite, so it actually executes.

Guidelines audited: docs/guidelines/TESTING.md, docs/guidelines/ERRORS.md,
docs/guidelines/CONTROL_FLOW.md, docs/guidelines/FUNCTIONS.md,
docs/guidelines/FILES.md, docs/guidelines/DESIGN_DOCS.md. Change complies.

